VDR Architecture & Flow Documentation
1. System Overview
This project is a high-security, desktop-based Virtual Data Room (VDR) viewer built using Electron, Node.js, and Supabase. It allows users to securely view and edit encrypted documents with zero-trust local storage, anti-screenshot protection, and strict audit logging.

2. Core Architecture (File Roles)
The application follows a strict 3-tier Electron architecture to ensure maximum security:

main.js (The Secure Backend): Runs in Node.js. It acts as the "Vault Keeper." It communicates with Supabase (Database & Storage), handles Fernet decryption/encryption, spawns the OS-level locked window, watches temporary files, and executes OS-level process kills (terminating MS Word/Excel).

preload.js (The Security Bridge): The IPC (Inter-Process Communication) bridge. It ensures the frontend UI can ask the backend to do things (like saving a file or checking out a document) without exposing sensitive Node.js APIs or Database Keys to the browser environment.

renderer.js (The UI Controller): The frontend JavaScript. It handles DOM manipulation, triggers the "Blur Shield" on Alt-Tab, manages button states (Edit, Save, Close), and handles the secure embedding of Microsoft's Online Viewer.

index.html (The View): The visual layout. It contains the locked Kiosk UI, login screens, and the secure iframe container for document rendering.

3. Encryption Standard
Protocol: Fernet (Symmetric Encryption based on AES 128-bit in CBC mode with SHA256 HMAC for authentication).

How it works: The entire file is converted to a Base64 string and encrypted as one massive, scrambled block.

Key Storage: The specific Fernet Key for each file is stored in the Supabase Database under the dek_ref column.

Limitation/Security Benefit: Because it is whole-file encryption, you cannot inject edits directly. Every save creates a completely new, unique encrypted file.

4. Feature & Database Flows (Step-by-Step)
A. View Flow (Opening a File)
Trigger: User selects a .vdr keycard file via the OS file browser.

Fetch: Electron reads the docId inside the .vdr file and queries the Supabase documents and document_permissions tables.

Verify: Checks if the user_id has can_read permissions.

Decrypt: Downloads the raw encrypted Blob from the vault-files bucket, decrypts it in memory using the dek_ref key via Fernet.

Render: * PDFs & TXT are rendered directly natively in the Electron window.

Office Files are rendered flawlessly by generating a 1-hour Temporary Signed URL from Supabase and passing it into a secure iframe running the Microsoft Office Online Viewer.

DB Update: Inserts a new row into document_access_logs recording the user_id, docId, and accessed_at timestamp.

B. Edit Flow: Pure Text (.txt)
Trigger: User clicks "Edit Text".

Action: The secure textarea unlocks for live typing.

Save: User clicks "Save". renderer.js safely extracts the Base64 text and passes it to main.js.

DB Update: * Encrypts the new text, uploads it as a brand new file (e.g., secret_v170000.txt).

Updates the documents table to point the file_path to this new version.

Deletes the old version from the bucket.

Logs the action in document_edit_logs.

C. Edit Flow: MS Office Checkout (.docx, .xlsx)
Trigger: User clicks "Edit in MS Office".

Checkout: main.js decrypts the file and saves it to a hidden OS temp folder, then forces Microsoft Word or Excel to open it natively.

Watch: Electron waits 3.5 seconds, then begins polling the file's Modified Time (mtimeMs) every 1 second.

Save & Auto-Kill: When the user hits Ctrl+S in MS Word:

Electron detects the new timestamp, grabs the fresh data, encrypts it, and uploads a new version to the vault.

DB Update: Updates file_path in documents and logs the event in document_edit_logs.

The Assassin: Electron executes taskkill /F /IM WINWORD.EXE to instantly force-close Microsoft Office.

Cleanup: Electron physically deletes the raw file from the hidden temp folder so no trace is left on the hard drive.

Reload: The UI refreshes to show the newly updated file.

D. Audit Logging (Closing a File)
Trigger: User clicks "Close File" or logs out.

Action: The frontend fires a "death signal" to main.js with the active logId.

DB Update: Updates the existing row in document_access_logs. It calculates the difference between now() and accessed_at, and populates closed_at, duration_sec (as an Integer), and duration_formatted (as a String like "5 min 30 sec").

5. Security Protocols
Anti-Screenshot: Uses Electron's native setContentProtection(true). Snipping Tool, OBS, and Zoom will capture a black rectangle.

Hardware Lock: Escape, F11, DevTools (Ctrl+Shift+I), and Refresh (Ctrl+R) are intercepted and ignored.

Blur Shield: If the user Alt-Tabs or clicks a secondary monitor, a massive red "SECURITY LOCK" screen covers the document instantly to prevent shoulder-surfing.

6. Supported Formats
Currently Supported & Tested: * PDF (Native view, highly secure)

TXT / HTML (Native view, Native live-edit)

DOCX, DOC, XLSX, XLS, CSV (Flawless view via MS Viewer, Native checkout editing)

Future Formats Supported (No code changes needed):

PPTX / PPT (PowerPoint natively supports the Checkout flow and MS Viewer).

Image Formats (PNG, JPG): Easily supported by routing Base64 data to an <img src=""> tag in renderer.js.

Future Formats (Requires new viewers):

MP4 / Video (Requires a secure streaming buffer chunker so the video isn't fully downloaded to memory at once).

CAD / DWG files (Would require Autodesk cloud viewers or similar WebGL integrations).